From 755fbf8bb3386b2a2b7ff20aa5316ebf250d564a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Profiler Workbench <profiler@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:03:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] asio: add USDT block markers and name the driver threads

A tracer can see Live's syscalls but cannot tell which audio block they
belong to or whether that block came in late. Add static trace points
(USDT, provider "pipeasio"; systemtap's sdt.h vendored under include/ with
the x86-64 site widened to a 5-byte nop) around the PipeWire cycle and the
host callback: cycle_start and cycle_end in audio_on_process, block_start,
block_before_live, block_after_live and block_end on the ASIO process path.
They are keyed by a per-instance block sequence and carry the cycle
deadline, PipeWire's next wakeup. Each site is one 5-byte nop plus an ELF
note, so nothing runs until a tracer attaches to a chosen process; the
probes are always compiled in and need no setting.

The deadline arithmetic lives in include/pipeasio_block_clock.h, shared
with telemetry: every sample now records the block sequence and its slack
against the deadline, and the per-second cycle line gains
block_range=A-B late=N so a tracer's view can be reconciled with the
driver's. The clock.position gap is measured before the host runs so the
block markers can report it; the xrun accounting itself is unchanged. The
data-loop thread is named pipeasio-rt and the lifecycle worker
pipeasio-worker, which Wine propagates to comm. The WoW64 PE front end
never sees sdt.h: PIPEASIO_PROBE is a no-op there.

Verification: test_block_clock passes with gcc 16 and clang 23 (24 checks,
also under ASan/UBSan). A probe-only unit built with the driver's flags
carries all six notes and 5-byte nops under gcc and clang, keeps them
through strip --strip-unneeded, and compiles to nothing with
PIPEASIO_WOW64_PE (also for an i686 mingw target). src/audio.c compiles in
the unixlib configuration against PipeWire 1.6.9 headers with gcc and
clang without new warnings; src/asio.c awaits the container build (no Wine
SDK on the check host).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
